
== research: reading about the topic on the web
  | **HOW IT WORKS** (the hook's answer: the alarm detects smoke *particles*, not the smoke you smell)
  | 1. A sensing chamber lets air in but blocks room light.
  | 2. Ionization type: a tiny americium-241 source (about 1/5000 g) ionizes the air between two plates, so a small steady current flows.
  | 3. Smoke particles attach to the ions, the current drops, and the circuit triggers.
  | 4. Photoelectric type: an LED beam normally misses the photodiode. Smoke scatters light onto it.
  | 5. The control IC sees the change, sounds the piezo buzzer (about 85 dB), and flashes the LED.
  | 
  | **REAL PARTS**
  | - Americium-241 source (ionization type)
  | - IR LED + photodiode (photoelectric type)
  | - Piezo buzzer
  | - Hobby alternative: MQ-2 gas/smoke sensor. It is not a certified alarm.
  | - I did not find a source for the detector IC part numbers, so I'm leaving them out.
  | 
  | **MYTH**
  | - Myth: "Alarms sense heat or smell."
  | - Truth: they sense particles. Ionization types react faster to flaming fires and photoelectric types to smouldering fires. The sources I found say NFPA recommends both technologies, or a single alarm certified to the current UL 217 standard.
  | 
  | **SURPRISING FACT**
  | - Replace the whole alarm 10 years after its manufacture date, not its purchase or installation date. The date is printed on the back of the unit.
  | 
  | **TIP**
  | - Test it monthly. If it chirps, replace the battery. If it still chirps with a fresh battery, or a sealed-battery unit chirps, replace the whole unit.
  | 
  | Sources:
  | - [Koorsen: ionization vs photoelectric](https://blog.koorsen.com/what-is-the-difference-between-ionization-and-photoelectric-smoke-alarms)
  | - [Safety+Health: when to replace](https://safetyandhealthmagazine.com/articles/14777-when-should-a-smoke-alarm-be-replaced-survey-shows-90-of-people-dont-know)

